{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791631306,"nonce":"LXacR7j57JNtvG2TsqrRQ1OPLWM=","title":"Text in a CSS-animated panel drifts from it, and fades or settles wrong","message":"In cn dropdowns and popovers (CSS `@keyframes` of opacity + scale + translateY, `transform-origin: top`), the text eases in and out at an offset that does not match its container. ashui never showed this.\n\n\nA CSS animation of opacity and transform alone was baked: the subtree was rasterized once into a `LayerTexture` and the compositor blitted it with translate/scale/opacity anchored at its top-left, ignoring `transform-origin`. Text, SVG, images and canvas are drawn in passes of their own and are not in that texture; text was re-anchored from baselines and so did not follow the blit. Offscreen (blinc_app GPU test), mid-animation the baked panel showed no text at all.\n\n\nA subtree holding anything but `Div`s is no longer baked; it is painted with the animation applied on every frame, as ashui does. That path had two defects of its own, which the change also fixes:\n\n1. The CSS-only patch fast path (`css_patch` in `try_render_with_compositor`) patches the cached primitives and reuses the text collected on the first frame. Text under a non-baked opacity animation stayed at that frame's alpha (0.04): invisible for the whole fade and after it. It now steps aside while a playing animation holds text, SVG, an image or a canvas (`RenderTree::css_active_patchable`).\n2. A finished animation was never written at its final values. `apply_all_css_animation_props` skips settled entries and relied on `apply_stylesheet_state_styles` to reset the node, which the windowed loop runs only when the hover/press/focus fingerprint changes, and which covers only elements with an id. The panel rested at opacity 0.96 until the mouse moved. Now the final values are written once when it stops (`css_anim_written` / `css_trans_written`), and `css_has_active` stays true until they are. Same for transitions.\n\nThe final values are the last keyframe's (as `fill-mode: forwards`), not the pre-animation base that `fill-mode: none` asks for. They agree for enter animations, which end at the resting value.\n\n\nOffscreen GPU test, 400 ms linear fade of a white panel with red text over black, 16 ms steps; alpha recovered from pixels (panel: grey / 255; text: 1 - min green under a glyph / grey):\n\n| Variant | mid panel | mid text | rest panel | rest text |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| baked (old rule) | 0.56 | ~0.56 | 1.0 | 1.0 |\n| not baked, no patch gate | 0.56 | none drawn | 0.96 | none drawn |\n| not baked, gate, no settle | 0.56 | 0.56 | 0.96 | 0.96 |\n| all three | 0.56 | 0.56 | 1.0 | 1.0 |\n\nScale about `top center` (400 ms, 0.5 -\u003e 1, translateY -20 -\u003e 0): with the old rule the text is absent mid-animation; with the fix the text sits at the same fraction of the panel mid-animation as at rest (within 0.04 x, 0.08 y).\n\n\n- `blinc_layout/tests/css_layer_bake.rs`: not baked with text or an SVG inside; still baked with nothing drawn apart; not patchable with text; final values written when the animation stops and `css_has_active` holds until then.\n- `blinc_app` `tests::an_animated_panel_carries_its_text`, `tests::an_animated_panel_fades_its_text` (GPU; skip without one).\n- Reverting any one of the three pieces fails a test.\n\n\nA dropdown or popover animation now takes the full paint path for its duration instead of the composite blit. Patching text and SVG under a non-baked CSS animation in `css_patch` (opacity and the same affine the panel gets) would let it keep the fast path.\n\nRelated: d1248f7 (tooltip text vanishes under a CSS fade-in) looks like the same first-frame text reuse.","files":null}]}